1	/**
2	 * declared-jobs.ts — one unit of work a project declares in `config.yaml`, validated.
3	 *
4	 * Five places in the configuration describe the same thing: a named piece of work,
5	 * the machine it runs on, the commands that are it, and the credentials those
6	 * commands may reach. They had three shapes. `checks.pull_request_runs` was a bare
7	 * list of commands in one job; `checks.default_branch_runs.jobs` was a list of named
8	 * jobs that could name secrets; `deploy` had targets with their own spelling again,
9	 * and one `secrets` list shared by every target. This is the one shape.
10	 *
11	 * ## What decides whether an entry may name a credential
12	 *
13	 * The tree the LIST is read from, and that is deliberately not a property of this
14	 * module. `checks` is read from the default branch, and the job an entry with a
15	 * credential lands in runs in that same tree; `deploy` is read from the branch or tag it
16	 * ships, after the merge. Neither is the change under review, so a credential named
17	 * beside an entry is safe in both -- and the question this module used to answer, which
18	 * arm may hold a key, went with the arms.
19	 *
20	 * What is left to remember is a name that would REPLACE something the job already has:
21	 * each list says which of those it reserves, and naming one is reported rather than
22	 * quietly dropped.
23	 *
24	 * ## What is NOT decided here
25	 *
26	 * When a job runs. A check runs on a pull request; a deployment runs on a merge, on a
27	 * tag, or when somebody asks. That belongs to whoever owns the list, because it is
28	 * the thing that differs between them — and folding it in here would put a
29	 * `branches:` key on a lint job.
30	 *
31	 * A list that owns extra keys says so through `extra`, which supplies both the names
32	 * — so everything else stays a typo — and the reader that turns them into fields.
33	 * `domain/delivery/deploy-jobs.ts` is the one caller that has any.
34	 *
35	 * Problems come back rather than throwing, and every entry is checked rather than
36	 * stopping at the first: somebody fixing their configuration should see everything
37	 * wrong with it.
38	 */
